data/kev-snapshot.json — known-exploited-vulnerability catalogue
---------------------------------------------------------------
Source:     CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog
Upstream:   https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog
Endpoint:   https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json
Licence:    CC0-1.0
Licence at: https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/licenses/kev/license.txt

CISA's own statement of the terms, quoted from that file:

    The KEV database is distributed under the Creative Commons 0 1.0 License.
    You may use this data in any legal manner but note that information
    provided at any 3rd party links included in the KEV database are bound by
    the policies and licenses of those 3rd party websites. Use of the
    information does not authorize you to use the CISA Logo or DHS Seal, nor
    should such use be interpreted as an endorsement by CISA or DHS.

CC0 asks for no attribution, so this entry exists for two other reasons: the
no-endorsement condition above is a real constraint on how the data may be
presented, and NOTICE is where the provenance of everything under data/ is
recorded. Nothing in ceproof or its documentation implies CISA or DHS
endorsement, and the CISA Logo and DHS Seal are not used anywhere.

What ceproof ships is a distillation, not a copy: per catalogue entry, only the
CVE id, CISA's vendor and product strings, the vulnerability name, the date
added, the remediation due date, the known-ransomware flag and the CWE ids. The
per-entry prose (shortDescription, requiredAction), the third-party advisory
links in `notes` — which CISA's licence text explicitly says are bound by other
sites' licences — and the federal-agency forensicTriage obligation are all
dropped. See scripts/fetch-kev-snapshot.ts for the full list and the reasons,
and the `source` block inside data/kev-snapshot.json for the SHA-256, catalogue
version and release date of the exact upstream response each snapshot was built
from.

The catalogue is CISA's record of vulnerabilities known to be exploited. It is
not a statement about any particular device, and a vendored snapshot goes out of
date by design — CISA adds entries continuously, and ceproof warns when the
bundled snapshot is more than 30 days old.

