data/sources/ — primary-source regulatory and standards text
-------------------------------------------------------------
Build-time only. NOT shipped in the published npm package (see
package.json's "files" allowlist and scripts/verify-pack.sh, which asserts its
absence from the tarball). Retained in the repository so every item in
data/cra-structure.json and data/fda-structure.json can be checked against the
text it was extracted from.

data/sources/fda-premarket-cybersecurity-2026-02-03-extract.md is a verbatim
pdftotext extract of FDA's "Cybersecurity in Medical Devices: Quality Management
System Considerations and Content of Premarket Submissions" (issued February 3,
2026; docket FDA-2021-D-1158, GUI00001825), from
https://www.fda.gov/media/119933/download. A work of the United States
Government, not subject to domestic copyright. Nothing in this repository is
endorsed by, affiliated with or reviewed by FDA.

data/fda-structure.json is this repository's own extraction of the content items
that guidance asks for, in the same form as data/cra-structure.json, and carries
no third-party content beyond the verbatim excerpts that make each item
traceable. The minimum SBOM elements it refers to are defined in
NTIA's "The Minimum Elements For a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)",
published July 12, 2021, at
https://www.ntia.gov/files/ntia/publications/sbom_minimum_elements_report.pdf
(title and date checked against ntia.gov on 2026-10-31). A work of the United
States Government, not subject to domestic copyright. FDA incorporates it by
reference, and it is NOT vendored in this repository — stated in
data/fda-structure.json's own `notVendored` field, which is why the seven
element names cannot be traced to vendored text here.

FDA's own sentence cites a DIFFERENT NTIA document by title — "Framing
Software Component Transparency: Establishing a Common Software Bill of
Materials (SBOM)", which FDA dates October 2021 — while asking for the
"minimum elements" the July 2021 report above sets out. Both are named,
verbatim where FDA is quoted, rather than one being silently preferred over
the other.
